WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.132
- [Yoko] Whether it's taking the subway,

00:00:03.132 --> 00:00:04.530
(man speaking in foreign language)

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.290
- [Yoko] Shopping for groceries.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:07.440
(machine speaking in foreign language)

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.210
- Or going into the office,

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.820
to do pretty much anything
in Beijing these days,

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.070
you're required to show this,

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.020
a negative COVID-19 test result

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.360
within the past 72 hours.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.430
So thousands of PCR testing booths

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:21.750
have popped up in Beijing

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.469
and other major cities across the country.

00:00:24.469 --> 00:00:27.450
(Mi speaking in foreign language)

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:28.530
- The goal?

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.800
To test people regularly in
order to spot the virus quickly

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.180
before it spreads.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.640
Plus, Beijing is back on high alert

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.613
as case numbers jumped in mid-June,

00:00:37.613 --> 00:00:40.770
despite the rollout of
thousands of swabbing booths.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.060
Beijing has been building

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.035
and staffing many of these testing sites

00:00:44.035 --> 00:00:47.100
and that raises concerns
about whether the strategy

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.410
is financially sustainable and effective

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.153
at stamping out the virus.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.510
Here's a testing booth.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.880
I walked around in this
area for about five minutes

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.430
and already found about
five testing booths,

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.410
and on this Chinese map app,

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.900
you can also find testing locations.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.300
In the three kilometers' distance,

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.580
you can see that there
are one, two, three, four,

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.273
five, six, seven, eight
PCR test centers around me.

00:01:19.482 --> 00:01:20.315
(Yoko speaking in foreign language)

00:01:20.315 --> 00:01:22.350
I just took a free PCR test as required

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.730
by the Beijing government

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:26.280
but making sure that everyone
gets tested frequently

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.320
and for free comes with a cost.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.300
The city government hasn't revealed

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.210
how much money has gone
into testing its population

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.430
of more than 21 million people

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.830
but based on the price set
by the central government

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.230
of about $1.20 per test,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.350
it would cost more than $25 million a day

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.873
to cover all of the
residents in the capital.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.340
Authorities have started rolling
out regular testing sites

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.710
in cities with high risks.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.050
But economists estimate
that if the whole country

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:58.080
were to adopt Beijing's
72-hour testing requirement,

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.680
it would cost up to 1.7% of the GDP.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.650
For comparison, China's
entire healthcare budget

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.983
in 2020 was about 7% of the GDP.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:12.270
And building a city-wide
network of testing sites

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:15.540
also requires investments in
staffing and construction.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.610
Many of the booths that I saw in Beijing

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.680
had at least two people working in it,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.840
and according to Chinese state media,

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.180
there are more than 9,000 booths like this

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.950
here in the capital.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.500
Some private companies
that operate some locations

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.000
are offering a monthly
salary of more $1,500.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.910
These stations also vary in quality.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.700
Some are made up of a few
folding chairs and tables

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:39.900
under a canopy,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.450
while others are more
solid structures outfitted

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.483
with plastic barriers,
gloves and air conditioners.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.470
All these booths tell
us that China's sticking

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:52.290
to its zero-COVID policy,
which is a approach

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.780
that's becoming rare
elsewhere in the world.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.780
Some analysts say China's
strategy of frequent testing

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.120
may be able to reduce some economic costs

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.370
by preventing city-wide lockdowns

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:05.010
but this approach can also
become a financial burden

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.960
for the government in the long run.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.510
China's top health authority
said it's necessary

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:13.230
to provide fast and high-quality
PCR testing services

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.110
in order to prevent the
virus from spreading widely.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:19.110
Still, it also said that
not every city needs

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:20.898
to do that many tests.

00:03:20.898 --> 00:03:25.065
(He speaking in foreign language)

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.100
- There are also questions

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.950
about the effectiveness of
requiring regular testing,

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.750
especially if some security guards

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:35.430
at entrances of public places

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.230
don't check the results closely

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.320
or some people just avoid
getting tested at all.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.140
China's approach means
that such frequent testing

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.250
and partial lockdowns

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:45.960
are going to be a part of people's lives

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.520
for the foreseeable future,

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.230
and that's a burden on the people,

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:51.030
especially as they start to realize

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.920
that the rest of the world has moved on

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.629
from such strict restrictions.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:57.379
(dramatic music)

